---
import Frame from '../components/PageFrame.astro';
const items = [
  {
    title: 'Human approval',
    body: 'A local state model holds the workflow at review until a person explicitly accepts the proposal.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Boundaries explained',
    body: 'Static content and browser-only fixtures make it clear what is working and what is conceptual.',
  },
  {
    title: 'No secret AI claims',
    body: 'Search is a local text match. Reports use prepared data. No AI provider or company account is connected.',
  },
];
---

<Frame label="Technology" items={items}>
  <section class="container section split-section" id="system">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">THE ACTUAL IMPLEMENTATION</p>
      <h2>Static where it helps. Interactive where it matters.</h2>
      <p>
        Astro renders the pages into HTML at build time. Shared styles and local
        fonts establish the visual system. A small TypeScript module enhances
        navigation, the product selector and the three local demos.
      </p>
      <p>
        Meaningful headings, explanations, links and sample documents arrive in
        the HTML. JavaScript adds interaction; it does not supply the only
        readable version of the site.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/compare/">
        Try the local interactions →
      </a>
    </div>
    <ol class="lifecycle">
      <li>
        <span class="number">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Source and content</h3>
          <p>
            A shared page registry controls identity and routes. Typed fixtures
            supply the documents, workflow roles and reporting series.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="number">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Static build</h3>
          <p>
            Astro produces a page for every route, plus metadata, the sitemap
            and a real not-found page.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="number">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Browser interaction</h3>
          <p>
            State updates stay local. Text nodes, native controls and explicit
            status messages keep the process inspectable.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
  <section class="dark-section">
    <div class="container split-section">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">VISION AND REALITY</p>
        <h2>A concept with an honest boundary.</h2>
        <p>
          A real operations system would need authentication, permissions,
          durable storage, recovery, audit history and tested integrations.
          Those services are outside this portfolio demonstration.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>What runs here</h3>
        <p>
          A four-stage local workflow, a fixed searchable document library and
          three selectable reporting fixtures.
        </p>
        <h3>What remains conceptual</h3>
        <p>
          Cross-product data sharing, automated external actions, AI retrieval
          and company data ingestion. No certifications, uptime guarantees or
          customer deployments are claimed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">EXPLAIN THE FLOW</p>
      <h2>From a request to a considered decision.</h2>
    </div>
    <img
      class="diagram"
      src="/images/diagrams/workflows-flow.svg"
      width="1200"
      height="340"
      loading="lazy"
      alt="Illustrative frontend stages: Intake, Prepare, Human review, Hand-off."
    />
    <p class="demo-note">
      This diagram is a conceptual model of the browser demo. It is not evidence
      of an external automation engine.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/guides/#privacy">
      Understand data and privacy boundaries →
    </a>
  </section>
</Frame>
